= The system of government

Spain is a parliamentary monarchy. The King proposes a candidate for Prime Minister after consulting the parties. The candidate needs an absolute majority of the Congress of Deputies (176 of 350) in a first vote, or more yes than no votes in a second vote 48 hours later. Abstentions therefore matter.

If no candidate is invested within two months of the first investiture vote, the Cortes are dissolved and a new election is held.

A government can be removed only by a constructive motion of no confidence: an absolute majority must vote for a named alternative Prime Minister, who takes office immediately.

The seventeen autonomous communities have their own parliaments and governments. Thirteen vote together each May with the municipal elections; Andalusia, Catalonia, the Basque Country and Galicia keep their own calendars.

@elections
go-to: menu

= How elections work

Each of Spain's 50 provinces is a constituency with at least two deputies; the other seats are shared by population. Ceuta and Melilla elect one each. Seats are allocated by the D'Hondt method, and a list needs 3% of the valid vote in a province, blank votes included.

Small provinces have few seats, so the system rewards parties that are strong everywhere and parties concentrated in one region, and penalises parties with thinly spread national support.
